<h3 id="planning-logistics-and-support-for-safe-streets-protests-and-rallies-breakout-topics"><a href="https://sachachua.com/blog/feed/index.xml#planning-logistics-and-support-for-safe-streets-protests-and-rallies-breakout-topics">Breakout topics</a></h3>
<div class="outline-text-3" id="text-planning-logistics-and-support-for-safe-streets-protests-and-rallies-breakout-topics">
<p>
Here were the general topics for open space conversations and some notes on next steps
(thanks to my husband for extra notes!):
</p>

<ul class="org-ul">
<li>rapid response: shift plan</li>
<li>organizing health care workers to protect our bike lanes: push media attention, union statements, op eds, journals</li>
<li>organizing parents to stand up for bike lanes: For Our Kids, virtual meeting, bike bus?</li>
<li>student organizing/University of Toronto Graduate Students’ Union (UTGSU): posters, stickers, town hall for students</li>
<li>Critical Mass organizing: new ride in May?</li>
<li>non-violent direct action: training on the 29th?</li>
<li>Bike Lanes 101: bring in new people, build people power, small group coffee chats, talk to the 905</li>
<li>engaging business: flip the script, owners, bia, consumers - be visible as cyclists, shopping passport</li>
<li>cycling winter: shovel bike lanes ourselves</li>
<li>connecting to opposition to highway 413</li>
<li>engineering reviews/critiques: will get drawings, make them public</li>
<li>future proofing: build few projects to high standards, accelerate construction, seamless network, push councillors, education, know what good infra is</li>
<li>federal level involvement: disallowance, cut bill 212, organize on ridings basis</li>
<li>website and digital infrastructure: Civic Tech Toronto</li>
<li>storytelling with data: isolate data for Bloor Yonge University lanes, bikeshare counts, ID core audience, biz owners, 905 drivers, common misconceptions, response, more data from city</li>
<li>guerrilla postering: creating a repository of art, setting up a mailing list</li>
<li>supplies and repairs during protest: coordinating supplies at the next protests/events</li>
<li>engaging drivers</li>
<li>organizing non-cyclists allies: open letter</li>
<li>safe complete streets: stop saying bike lanes, say complete streets, beautify, personal stories over stats</li>
<li>how to organize provincially</li>
<li>pressuring the city to not police protests: strategic mtg with councillors, pressure mayor, reduce police budget, testimonials from arrested</li>
<li>transit: TTC integration, free bike share pass?</li>
</ul>

<p>
More wrap-ups:
