<div id="outline-container-planning-logistics-and-support-for-safe-streets-protests-and-rallies-parenting-families" class="outline-3">
<h3 id="planning-logistics-and-support-for-safe-streets-protests-and-rallies-parenting-families"><a href="https://sachachua.com/blog/feed/index.xml#planning-logistics-and-support-for-safe-streets-protests-and-rallies-parenting-families">Parenting, families</a></h3>
<div class="outline-text-3" id="text-planning-logistics-and-support-for-safe-streets-protests-and-rallies-parenting-families">
<p>
I participated in the conversation on organizing parents to stand up
for bike lanes.
We talked about making protests and rallies friendlier for families
and kids through hot chocolate, popsicles, bubbles, stickers, bike
decoration stations, and other activities.
People also exchanged ideas about organizing bike buses to help kids
bike to school, meeting up with other cyclists at bike-friendly cafes
during parental leave, and promoting bike education.
We talked about widening the conversation from a focus on bike lanes
to safety for everyone and connecting to what people care about,
especially through personal stories and concrete life changes.
</p>

<p>
One of the triggers for the shift towards a stronger cycling culture in the Netherlands was collective outrage over so many child deaths in traffic accidents.
I would like to get to that point without as many individual tragedies.
</p>

<ul class="org-ul">
<li><a href="https://www.theguardian.com/cities/2015/may/05/amsterdam-bicycle-capital-world-transport-cycling-kindermoord">How Amsterdam became the bicycle capital of the world | Cities | The Guardian</a></li>
<li><a href="https://www.bbc.com/news/magazine-23587916">Why is cycling so popular in the Netherlands? - BBC News</a></li>
</ul>

<p>
It's challenging to go to protests and rallies when you have to consider kids in tow.
They get cold and hungry and bored and tired.
They need to go to the bathroom. They need to be safe, especially when there's anger or aggression from people on the road or online. I wonder what can make it easier for families to come.
</p>

<p>
My husband and I have been going to quite a few of these events
together with our 10-year-old. She likes offering people hot
chocolate, snacks, and hand warmers: little ways to care for people
who care about everyone's safety. I want to connect with other people
so that we can figure out together how to make it easier for families
to get involved. I am totally willing to start with hot chocolate and stickers. =)
</p>

<p>
Resources:
</p>

<ul class="org-ul">
<li><a href="https://www.forourkids.ca/">For Our Kids</a></li>
<li><a href="https://www.ffsafestreets.ca/">Friends and Families for Safe Streets</a></li>
</ul>
</div>
