<div id="outline-container-planning-logistics-and-support-for-safe-streets-protests-and-rallies-supplies-and-repairs-for-supporting-protests" class="outline-3">
<h3 id="planning-logistics-and-support-for-safe-streets-protests-and-rallies-supplies-and-repairs-for-supporting-protests"><a href="https://sachachua.com/blog/feed/index.xml#planning-logistics-and-support-for-safe-streets-protests-and-rallies-supplies-and-repairs-for-supporting-protests">Supplies and repairs for supporting protests</a></h3>
<div class="outline-text-3" id="text-planning-logistics-and-support-for-safe-streets-protests-and-rallies-supplies-and-repairs-for-supporting-protests">
<p>
I was surprised when our normally quite reserved 10-year-old suggested this open space breakout session herself, standing up in
front of hundreds of people to invite them to a conversation about how
to support protests through supplies and bike repairs. When she talked to the coordinator, she opted for a small space for about 5 people, but we ended up with about 10. She reported the results back to the main audience herself, too.
</p>

<p>
It's surprising how little things can go a long way to helping people
feel more supported and taken care of. Our experience started by going
to protests and offering people water refills, because it's important
to stay hydrated. Most people have their own water bottles, but they
might run out over the course of a few hours. We have cargo bikes, so
my husband brings this huge container of water and refills people's
bottles. As the weather got colder, we shifted to bringing warm water
too. We're used to making hot chocolate for a crowd because it's a good
way to keep kids warm during an outdoor play date. It's actually
pretty delightful to see even adults light up when you offer them
marshmallows with their cocoa. My husband's also been bringing bike
repair tools, since quite a few people had to deal with flat tires
from debris in contested bike lanes. Other people have even set up
bike stands for check-ups and repairs. Other people have brought hand
warmers and snacks. They pitched in with a variety of masks when the
machines were grinding up the road surface. Little things help.
</p>

<p>
Sometimes having a list of ideas makes it easier for people to find one or two things they can contribute.
We brainstormed a lot of little things that could help at these events:
</p>

<ul class="org-ul">
<li>table with snacks &amp; info</li>
<li>warmth, water, and food
<ul class="org-ul">
<li>hot cocoa</li>
<li>hand warmers</li>
<li>popsicles in summer</li>
<li>water: water bottles, refills, hot water</li>
<li>pizza, samosas, etc.</li>
</ul></li>
<li>tools
<ul class="org-ul">
<li>nametags? totally optional</li>
<li>signs, flyers, sign-making station</li>
<li>masks, PPE, first aid</li>
<li>bike repairs: tubes, tools → reach out to bike shops</li>
</ul></li>
<li>info
<ul class="org-ul">
<li>info, contact, sign up</li>
<li>zines</li>
<li>sign-ups to coordinate with Bike Lane Watch?</li>
<li>nearby support sites: where to find bathrooms, nearby bike-friendly businesses to recommend</li>
</ul></li>
<li>fun
<ul class="org-ul">
<li>kid activities, games, fidgets</li>
<li>stickers, sticker trading</li>
</ul></li>
<li>other ideas
<ul class="org-ul">
<li>music</li>
<li>candles</li>
<li>someone to lock up and retrieve a bike if people get arrested</li>
<li>glow sticks, lights, batteries, chargers - Green P: power?</li>
<li>introvert corner</li>
<li>camp chairs, mats, tables</li>
<li>ways to reduce packaging, trash</li>
<li>moral support</li>
</ul></li>
<li>$ funds
<ul class="org-ul">
<li>individuals sharing</li>
<li>tip jar?</li>
<li>that GoFundMe?</li>
</ul></li>
</ul>

<p>
It might be nice to figure out how we can coordinate with other people who are thinking of bringing things or ordering things so that
for example, if we're picking up pizza, we don't end up with too much pizza. Maybe the Telegram chat?
</p>

<p>
Sometimes the protest is on a sidewalk where there's not a lot of space to set up a table or cargo bikes.
It might be a good idea to identify beforehand a park or a side street
where we can set up some more support,
so that people can go there to take a break before they go back into the fray.
For example, when the protests were at Islington,
we stayed at Tom Riley Park, and people came by to refuel as needed.
</p>

<p>
Is there funding? Quite a few people have offered, although it feels a little complicated.
There has been some kind of fundraiser, but we haven't coordinated anything with them.
People have also offered donations or suggested having a tip jar.
For us, I think we're more comfortable just sharing what we can share as a gift (and avoiding liability! =) ).
I love how other people have, on their own initiative, brought samosas and naan bread and pizza and sesame crisps and Timbits. More potluck than catering.
</p>

<p>
People suggested putting together some pre-packed boxes so that
they're easy to grab and go as part of a rapid response to the
anticipated bike lane removals next spring. Maybe that's like how we
keep a large bag with cups, marshmallows, stir sticks, and hot
chocolate mix, so that could be a starting point.
</p>
